<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Itzamna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Itzamna was one of the principal gods of the Maya, a creator deity associated with the sky, writing, knowledge and healing, often shown as an old man. At the time of the Spanish conquest he was honoured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izamal">Izamal</a> in northern Yucatán, a pilgrimage centre with great pyramids. Spanish friars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a> recorded his worship.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/itzamna.jpg" alt="Itzamna" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown Maya artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:God_D_Itzamna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
